{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n     6\t\n     7\t## Core Philosophy\n     8\t\n     9\tEverything is data.\n    10\tInterpretation > abstraction.\n    11\tExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n    12\tRestrictions are a feature.\n    13\t\n    14\t### Foundational Axioms\n    15\t\n    16\t1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n    17\t2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n    18\t3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n    19\t4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n    20\t\n    21\t## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n    22\t\n    23\t- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n    24\t- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n    25\t- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n    26\t- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n    27\t- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n    28\t- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Tuples and Datoms\n    31\t\n    32\tTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n    33\tA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n    34\tSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n    35\t\n    36\tSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n    37\t\n    38\t## Streams\n    39\t\n    40\tAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n    41\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    42\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    43\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    44\tStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n    45\t\n    46\tA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n    47\tof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n    48\tsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n    49\tstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n    50\tthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n    51\tanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n    52\texists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n    53\tthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n    54\tand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n    55\tbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n    56\tthe stream's own.\n    57\t\n    58\tFunctions can take anything and return anything.\n    59\tAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n    60\t\n    61\tSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n    62\tSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n    63\t\n    64\t### Host Boundaries\n    65\t\n    66\tA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\n    67\tsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\n    68\texhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n    69\t\n    70\t**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\n    71\thappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\n    72\tappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\n    73\tidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\n    74\tno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\n    75\tcorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n    76\t\n    77\t**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\n    78\ta timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\n    79\tnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n    80\tConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\n    81\tto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n    82\t\n    83\t**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\n    84\tadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\n    85\ttimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\n    86\twhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\n    87\tthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\n    88\tto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\n    89\tdeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\n    90\tmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\n    91\tnot an upward callback.\n    92\t\n    93\tHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\n    94\tplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\n    95\ttransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\n    96\tadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\n    97\tevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\n    98\tfor facts it owns.\n    99\t\n   100\tWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n   101\tadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n   102\ttransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\n   103\tthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\n   104\tconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n   105\t\n   106\tA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n   107\tnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\n   108\thappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\n   109\tevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n   110\t(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\n   111\tdoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\n   112\tnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\n   113\tinterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n   114\t\n   115\tTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\n   116\tcall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\n   117\tappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\n   118\tcallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\n   119\tthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\n   120\tdirections wearing the shape of an event.\n   121\t\n   122\t## Architectural Commitments\n   123\t\n   124\t- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n   125\t- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n   126\t- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n   127\t- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n   128\t\n   129\t## Design Principles\n   130\t\n   131\tCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\n   132\tStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n   133\t\n   134\t**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\n   135\tcanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\n   136\tshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n   137\t`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\n   138\tquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\n   139\tconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\n   140\tretired `:global` tag in\n   141\t[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\n   142\tcase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n   143\t\n   144\tSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n   145\tSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n   146\t\n   147\t## Code Style\n   148\t\n   149\t- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n   150\t- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n   151\t- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n   152\t- Avoid cleverness.\n   153\t- Name things after what they do to streams.\n   154\t\n   155\t## Development Workflow\n   156\t\n   157\t- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n   158\t- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n   159\t- Do not optimize prematurely.\n   160\t- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n   161\t- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n   162\t- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n   163\t  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n   164\t  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n   165\t  leaves to review.\n   166\t\n   167\t### Problem Decomposition\n   168\t\n   169\tIf a problem appears complex:\n   170\t1. Decompose it into streams.\n   171\t2. Identify invariants.\n   172\t3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n   173\t4. Make causality explicit.\n   174\t5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n   175\t\n   176\t## Operational & Specialized Guides\n   177\t\n   178\t- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n   179\t- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n   180\t- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n   181\t- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n   182\t- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoLease\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: implemented (`src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc`, `test/dao/lease_test.cljc`,\n     4\t`test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc`; merged 2026-09-20), derived from and\n     5\tsubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). The three use cases in\n     6\t[`dao.lease.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.lease.implementation-plan.md) are\n     7\tsketches, not finished components. This document is the operative contract:\n     8\tevery sentence below is a rule. Why the design is this shape is in\n     9\t[`dao.lease.rationale.md`](./dao.lease.rationale.md), which binds nothing.\n    10\t\n    11\tA lease is a grant that lapses unless renewed. It adds no operation to any\n    12\tcontract and no key to any DaoStream result map, leaving\n    13\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) unchanged by its existence.\n    14\t\n    15\t## Vocabulary\n    16\t\n    17\tFacts are plain data on ordinary streams, classified by two dispatch keys:\n    18\t`:dao.lease/status` for the negotiation, `:dao.lease/event` for evidence. A\n    19\treader switches on both. A fact carrying neither is not a lease fact and is\n    20\tignored; a fact carrying both is defective.\n    21\t\n    22\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    23\t| Fact     | `:dao.lease/status`   | Author  | Identity carried                            | Also required                                                             |\n    24\t+==========+=======================+=========+=============================================+===========================================================================+\n    25\t| Proposal | `:dao.lease/proposed` | holder  | `:dao.lease/proposal`                       | `:dao.lease/subject`; `:dao.lease/duration` optional, an ask              |\n    26\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    27\t| Grant    | `:dao.lease/accepted` | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`, plus                    | `:dao.lease/subject`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:dao.lease/duration`;         |\n    28\t|          |                       |         | `:dao.lease/proposal` if answering one      | `:dao.lease/max` optional                                                 |\n    29\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    30\t| Refusal  | `:dao.lease/rejected` | grantor | `:dao.lease/proposal`                       | —                                                                         |\n    31\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    32\t| Release  | `:dao.lease/released` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`                          | —                                                                         |\n    33\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    34\t| Reclaim  | `:dao.lease/lapsed`   | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`                          | `:dao.lease/cause` — `:silence`, `:release`, `:cap`, or `:policy`         |\n    35\t+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    36\t\n    37\t+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n    38\t| Evidence   | `:dao.lease/event`   | Author  | Identity carried     | Also required        |\n    39\t+============+======================+=========+======================+======================+\n    40\t| Renewal    | `:dao.lease/renewal` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`   | —                    |\n    41\t+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n    42\t| Tick       | `:dao.lease/tick`    | adapter | —                    | `:dao.lease/reading` |\n    43\t+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n    44\t\n    45\t**Keys.**\n    46\t\n    47\t- `:dao.lease/lease` — the lease's identity, minted by the grantor, carried by\n    48\t  every fact about it, never reused within that grantor.\n    49\t- `:dao.lease/proposal` — a proposal's identity, minted by the holder, echoed\n    50\t  by the grant or refusal answering it.\n    51\t- `:dao.lease/subject` — what is leased, in whatever plain data the domain uses.\n    52\t- `:dao.lease/holder` — the party whose renewals count, in whatever form the\n    53\t  medium's attribution takes.\n    54\t- `:dao.lease/duration` — a single-entry map from a unit keyword to a positive\n    55\t  integer magnitude. `:dao.lease/max`, `:dao.lease/reading` and a judge's\n    56\t  tolerance use the same representation; tolerance may be zero. A magnitude\n    57\t  stays within the bound that keeps every comparison and sum of the\n    58\t  composition's units exact on every host — the reference bound is 2^52 raw\n    59\t  and, against a supplied table, `quot 2^52 unit-magnitude`; a magnitude past\n    60\t  it establishes nothing.\n    61\t- `:dao.lease/cause` — why a reclaim happened.\n    62\t\n    63\t**Units.** The unit keywords and their ratios are a composition's\n    64\tinteroperability decision, fixed once and shared by its grantor, holders and\n    65\tjudge. Comparison normalizes to the finer of two units, and is strict: an\n    66\tinterval exactly equal to its bound has not yet passed it.\n    67\t\n    68\t**Validity.** A fact is defective, and establishes nothing, when it omits a key\n    69\tits status or event requires; carries a status or event outside the tables\n    70\tabove; carries a duration, cap or reading that is not a single-entry map of a\n    71\tfixed unit to a positive integer; carries a lease id on a proposal; answers one\n    72\tproposal with both `:accepted` and `:rejected`, or repeats `:accepted`,\n    73\t`:released` or `:lapsed` for one lease; or carries a reading older than one\n    74\talready observed. A judge reading a defective fact has read no lease fact.\n    75\t\n    76\t## Authority\n    77\t\n    78\t- **Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself\n    79\t  tenure. A holder fact establishes no term; a delayed holder renewal is still\n    80\t  evidence and counts as such.\n    81\t- **A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**\n    82\t  A renewal from any other author is a fact about that author, and silence is\n    83\t  measured as though it had not arrived.\n    84\t- **At most one of `:accepted` or `:rejected` answers a proposal.**\n    85\t  Renegotiating is a new proposal with a new proposal identity.\n    86\t- **A proposal creates no state.** Nothing exists until `:accepted`. A grantor\n    87\t  owes no answer, and a holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by\n    88\t  nothing in this vocabulary.\n    89\t- **A grantor may grant unsolicited**, with no proposal, in which case the\n    90\t  holder is the party the grant is delivered to.\n    91\t- **A `:policy` cause may not consist of declining to count evidence\n    92\t  received.** Eligible renewals are counted whether or not another condition\n    93\t  ends the lease.\n    94\t\n    95\t## Prohibitions\n    96\t\n    97\t- A lease may not extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no\n    98\t  DaoStream operation may consult a lease.\n    99\t- No timer holds a callback and no notification marks a lapse.\n   100\t- No absolute time appears in any fact.\n   101\t- A reclaim frees the resource, never the record.\n   102\t- A holder renews from its own control flow. No machinery renews on its behalf.\n   103\t\n   104\t## Time\n   105\t\n   106\tTime reaches lease code only as data; neither judge nor holder reads a host\n   107\tclock. Both read a tick stream their composition wires. Readings on one stream\n   108\tnever decrease, and a composition persisting a ledger across a restart keeps\n   109\tits readings comparable across it.\n   110\t\n   111\tA tick-stream gap makes a pass late, not wrong; a lease-fact gap is governed\n   112\tunder *Judging with incomplete evidence*.\n   113\t\n   114\t## The judge\n   115\t\n   116\tThe judge is composed **inside the boundary that possesses the resource** and\n   117\treclaims by acting on what it itself holds; one that does not possess the\n   118\tresource is outside this contract. It concludes and reclaims in one\n   119\tinterpreter, which is not the forbidden collapse: it is the possessing host\n   120\tinterpreter performing its own act.\n   121\t\n   122\t**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease:\n   123\t\n   124\t- its **terms** — subject, holder, duration, cap;\n   125\t- its **tenure start**, the reading the grant was seeded at, which no renewal\n   126\t  changes;\n   127\t- its **last relevant observation**, the reading of the grant or newest\n   128\t  eligible renewal, whichever is later;\n   129\t- its **evidence state** — `known`, or `unknown` with its resumed reading;\n   130\t- its **reclaim state** — `live`, or `pending` carrying the classified cause\n   131\t  and whether the reclaim succeeded.\n   132\t\n   133\tA lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act,\n   134\tand **leaves** once its reclaim has succeeded and its `:lapsed` record has been\n   135\tappended with `:dao.stream/ok`. Until both, it remains.\n   136\t\n   137\tThe ledger is not rebuildable from any stream: a composition either persists it,\n   138\tas a stream its owner writes and reads, or recovers by the rule under *Restart*.\n   139\t\n   140\t### The pass\n   141\t\n   142\tA pass classifies nothing until a first tick has been observed. At each pass,\n   143\tin this order:\n   144\t\n   145\t1. **Drain every wired tick cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`. The newest\n   146\t   reading drained, or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is\n   147\t   **now** for the whole pass.\n   148\t2. **Drain every wired lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping\n   149\t   each fact with that same *now*, and apply it: an eligible renewal advances\n   150\t   the lease's last relevant observation to *now* and returns it to `known`; a\n   151\t   valid `:released` from the holder marks it released; a `:dao.stream/gap`\n   152\t   marks every lease on that medium `unknown` with *now* as its resumed\n   153\t   reading. Each cursor's drain may be bounded by a composition-declared\n   154\t   budget; a cursor still yielding when the budget is spent has not completed\n   155\t   its observation, and while any wired fact cursor is so truncated, the pass\n   156\t   classifies no `:silence` for the leases that truncated medium carries —\n   157\t   `:release`, `:cap`, `:policy` and pending retries are never suppressed. An\n   158\t   incomplete drain may delay a reclaim; it may never manufacture one.\n   159\t3. **Answer and grant.** Proposals drained this pass may be answered, and\n   160\t   unsolicited grants authored, both seeded and stamped with this pass's *now*.\n   161\t   A grant authored between passes is seeded with the next pass's *now*.\n   162\t4. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds, and the\n   163\t   first that holds in this order is its cause: it is already `pending`, and\n   164\t   keeps the cause it carries; a valid `:released` from its holder has been\n   165\t   observed, `:release`; its tenure start is further back than\n   166\t   `:dao.lease/max`, `:cap`; the grantor's own policy ends it, `:policy`; its\n   167\t   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus\n   168\t   tolerance, `:silence`. A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for\n   169\t   `:silence` only once, in addition, a full duration has passed since its\n   170\t   resumed reading.\n   171\t5. **Reclaim** each due lease by its composition-supplied procedure, marking it\n   172\t   `pending` with its cause first. The procedure is idempotent and reports\n   173\t   whether it succeeded; a reclaim that fails or does not report leaves the\n   174\t   lease `pending` and unrecorded.\n   175\t6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\n   176\t   whose reclaim succeeded. On `:dao.stream/ok` the lease leaves the ledger;\n   177\t   otherwise it stays `pending` and the next pass reaches it again through the\n   178\t   first clause of step 4.\n   179\t\n   180\tThe record follows the act and never precedes it. **The absence of a `:lapsed`\n   181\tfact is never evidence of tenure.**\n   182\t\n   183\tA drain that ends in `:dao.stream/end` retires that cursor for subsequent\n   184\tpasses. A drain that ends in `:dao.stream/transport-error` ends the pass before\n   185\tstep 4.\n   186\t\n   187\t### Judging with incomplete evidence\n   188\t\n   189\tAbsence is evidence only over a window the judge observed. After a\n   190\t`:dao.stream/gap`, a lease with no renewal in the retained tail is `unknown`,\n   191\tand a surviving older renewal is not inferred to be the newest. A lease\n   192\trecovered from a persisted ledger is `unknown`. Conditions other than\n   193\t`:silence` apply to an `unknown` lease unchanged.\n   194\t\n   195\t### Restart\n   196\t\n   197\tA grantor that has lost its ledger **reclaims and re-grants**: for each\n   198\tresource it still possesses it performs the reclaim first, then grants afresh.\n   199\tThe reclaim ends the prior tenure; a new grant alone does not.\n   200\t\n   201\tThis requires an inventory of possessed resources and a recoverable holder for\n   202\teach, both of which belong to the resource rather than to this vocabulary.\n   203\tA grantor with neither persists its ledger instead.\n   204\t\n   205\t## The holder\n   206\t\n   207\t- **Observe the grant before acting**, and establish that the grantor authored\n   208\t  it by the same attribution its composition supplies to the judge. A holder\n   209\t  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\n   210\t- **Renew at less than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own\n   211\t  tick stream. A renewal is an append returning `:dao.stream/ok`; any other\n   212\t  outcome does not advance the holder's bound.\n   213\t- **Stop acting at the bound** — the earlier of the duration since the later of\n   214\t  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\n   215\t  whether or not anything has been heard.\n   216\t- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records\n   217\t  it.\n   218\t- **Treat an unusable reading as unanswerable.** A reading that is not a\n   219\t  single-entry `{unit, positive integer magnitude}` within the composition's\n   220\t  table answers nothing: every holder discipline fails closed on it, and a\n   221\t  flow that guards on its bound alone does not act.\n   222\t\n   223\tFor cap purposes, the holder measures the cap interval from the reading at\n   224\twhich it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor\n   225\tjudge's tenure start. The holder's observation-flight interval does not\n   226\textend, delay, or alter the judge's cap or reclaim decision; a resource\n   227\trequiring exclusion must enforce it through fencing.\n   228\t\n   229\tThe bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it\n   230\tfrom the resource.\n   231\t\n   232\t## Composition duties\n   233\t\n   234\tA composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\n   235\t\n   236\t- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime driving it at a declared cadence** —\n   237\t  the maximum interval between *completed* passes.\n   238\t- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for every holder.\n   239\t- **A tolerance** supplied to the judge, possibly zero.\n   240\t- **An attribution resolver** for the judge and for every holder observing a\n   241\t  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\n   242\t  envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity, since DaoStream supplies\n   243\t  none. One handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is refused\n   244\t  at assembly.\n   245\t- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n   246\t- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\n   247\t  recipient reads its carried facts from.\n   248\t- **Media that either retain without eviction or declare evict-oldest**,\n   249\t  refused at assembly otherwise.\n   250\t- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\n   251\t  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\n   252\t  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n   253\t  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\n   254\t\n   255\t## Sizing\n   256\t\n   257\tStated as relations; the values belong to compositions.\n   258\t\n   259\t- The granted duration exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.\n   260\t- The judge's tolerance covers expected flight time and the rate skew between\n   261\t  its tick stream and the holder's.\n   262\t- A medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag — cadence plus drain time.\n   263\t- A cap below the duration is permitted, and tenure then ends at the cap.\n   264\t- The holder's renewal interval plus one period of its tick stream is\n   265\t  strictly below half the granted duration: a renewal lands at the first\n   266\t  reading at or past the interval, up to one period late, so the\n   267\t  before-half guarantee is bought against interval plus period.\n   268\t\n   269\t## Limits\n   270\t\n   271\t- **A false lapse is possible.** Delay errs against the holder while a renewal\n   272\t  is unobserved and for it once observed, so a late-observed renewal from a\n   273\t  dead holder buys a fresh duration, and continuity is lost when none is\n   274\t  observed before a completed pass finds the interval past duration plus\n   275\t  tolerance. Partition length alone decides neither.\n   276\t- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one ledger lifetime.**\n   277\t- **A medium that gaps more often than one duration** never completes an\n   278\t  observation window and leaks continuously while every component reports\n   279\t  healthy; per-attachment media are the isolation.\n   280\t- **Reclamation is bounded by** duration plus tolerance plus cadence plus the\n   281\t  reclaim's own cost, given an uninterrupted observation window.\n   282\t\n   283\t## Carriage\n   284\t\n   285\tProposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\n   286\tpayload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\n   287\tis delivery, not a second authoring.\n   288\t\n   289\t`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\n   290\tremote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\n   291\tordinary `:ws/closed`; for a stream served under `dao.stream.remote.md`, the\n   292\t`not-found` protocol error its mirror answers for the reclaimed identity. A remote holder that\n   293\treads the grantor's stream through a served handle is observing the grantor's\n   294\trecord, not receiving carriage. No close code is assigned here; distinguishing\n   295\treclaim on the wire belongs to `dao.stream.ws.md`'s deferred close-code design.\n   296\t\n   297\t## Out of scope\n   298\t\n   299\t- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author.\n   300\t- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**\n   301\t- **Who may be granted anything at all**, and **what is leasable** — this\n   302\t  contract gates nothing, and the domain decides. `dao.stream.remote.md`\n   303\t  names two subjects: a stream entry served for a remote party, and a relay\n   304\t  pair on a meeting peer.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
