{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '295,430p'\"","aggregated_output":"   295\tthe event writer.\n   296\t\n   297\t### 2.5 Loss and resend\n   298\t\n   299\tThe reflection reads no clock. A link's `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\n   300\tmeans: an outstanding `descriptor`, `cursor` or `next` request is re-sent\n   301\twhen the caller has asked `k` further times and it is still unanswered.\n   302\t`k` is unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small on UDP. Those\n   303\tthree operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\n   304\tor a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent:\n   305\tdeduplication and correlation are the payload's (OD-2, accepted), and an\n   306\tappend whose answer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the\n   307\tevent writer as `{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n   308\t:dao.stream.remote/id n}` when the reflection is closed or the channel ends.\n   309\tThere are no sessions, no acknowledgement vectors, no windows and no result\n   310\tretention at the answering peer: the cursor is the sequence number for\n   311\treads, and `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel artifact.\n   312\t\n   313\t## 3. Channels\n   314\t\n   315\tA channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n   316\tis ordered and reliable; the link reads the last as composition data for\n   317\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, and nothing else in section 2 changes\n   318\tper channel.\n   319\t\n   320\t### 3.1 WebSocket\n   321\t\n   322\t`dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md` is the socket layer, and\n   323\ta channel is one of its attachments plus a projection. **Reused**: the\n   324\tattachment model and identity; the adapter that deposits every event as\n   325\t`{:ws/attachment id :ws/event e :ws/value v}` on the medium the composition\n   326\twired; the writer handle, whose `append!` frames each value as one\n   327\t`{:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value v}` message in the negotiated codec profile\n   328\t(Transit-JSON text or the CBOR binary profile); the bounded acceptance\n   329\thandoff with slot and acknowledgement; the resolution and lifecycle\n   330\tvocabulary. Ordered and reliable; frame budget unbounded.\n   331\t\n   332\t**New, the projection.** The mirror and the link speak raw request and\n   333\tanswer maps, so each channel end composes one step, `ws-project`, between\n   334\tthe ws deposit medium and the channel. The channel reader is a ring buffer\n   335\tthe composition wires; the channel writer is the ws handle itself.\n   336\t`ws-project` holds the reading cursor on the attachment's traffic medium,\n   337\tkeeps the events whose `:ws/attachment` names this channel, appends the\n   338\t`:ws/value` of each `:ws/payload` onto the ring buffer, and drops\n   339\t`:ws/error` diagnostics. A terminal lifecycle event for the attachment\n   340\t(`:ws/closed`, `:ws/ended`) or a failure resolution (`:ws/not-found`,\n   341\t`:ws/transport-error`) makes `ws-project` close the ring buffer: channel\n   342\tloss is then the link's `end` observation (2.4). `ws-project` is a step the\n   343\tcomposition drives at its own cadence, as it drives `endpoint-step`.\n   344\t\n   345\tDirection is establishment, not authority. A WebSocket has a dialer and an\n   346\tacceptor because TCP does. Once established the channel is symmetric and\n   347\tnothing in this document remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs on Node and\n   348\tcljd may dial or accept (`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`,\n   349\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`);\n   350\tcljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n   351\t\n   352\t### 3.2 UDP\n   353\t\n   354\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\n   355\tretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\n   356\tdatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\n   357\tvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n   358\t\n   359\t- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n   360\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   361\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   362\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   363\t- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. The budget covers the\n   364\t  whole encoded datagram, fragment envelope included: at most 1200 bytes,\n   365\t  the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path MTUs.\n   366\t- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n   367\t  split into `k` datagrams, each\n   368\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n   369\t  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n   370\t  id. The receiver reassembles by `[channel attachment identity,\n   371\t  source-address, direction, id]`, because ids are unique only per channel\n   372\t  reader and one socket may serve several attachments, and delivers only a\n   373\t  complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole message,\n   374\t  recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by two\n   375\t  composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB)\n   376\t  and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest partial\n   377\t  message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a fragment.\n   378\t- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n   379\t  in a payload.\n   380\t- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n   381\t  punching depends on it (section 4).\n   382\t- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n   383\t  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n   384\t  republishes.\n   385\t\n   386\tHosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs on Node (`dgram`), cljd\n   387\t(`RawDatagramSocket`). Browsers have no UDP.\n   388\t\n   389\t### 3.3 Pair\n   390\t\n   391\tA pair descriptor names two remote descriptors:\n   392\t\n   393\t```clojure\n   394\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/pair\n   395\t :dao.stream/identity  <channel identity>\n   396\t :dao.stream.remote/in  <remote descriptor>    ; this peer reads here\n   397\t :dao.stream.remote/out <remote descriptor>}   ; this peer writes here\n   398\t```\n   399\t\n   400\t`attach!` on it returns a channel whose reader is a reflection of `in` and\n   401\twhose writer is a reflection of `out`. When a third peer serves both\n   402\tstreams from its table, two peers that cannot reach each other have a\n   403\tchannel, and the third peer runs nothing but mirror steps over two ring\n   404\tbuffers; the requests and answers inside are values in its streams it never\n   405\tinterprets. Streams are values sent through streams. Ordered; best-effort\n   406\tunder the inboxes' retention. **An `in` gap ends the channel.** The pair\n   407\tadapter holds the reading cursor on `in`; when `next` there answers `gap`,\n   408\tframes were lost and the adapter cannot say which, so it closes the channel\n   409\treader for this link. The link observes `end` and runs its channel-loss\n   410\tpath (2.4): outstanding ids are abandoned, not re-sent, each abandoned\n   411\t`append!` is reported `append-unknown`, and reattachment is the caller's,\n   412\tby `attach!` on the same pair descriptor, which mints a fresh reading\n   413\tcursor on `in` at `:newest`. The gap is never reported to an inner reader\n   414\tas a source's `gap`. Double framing consumes budget: an inner value inside\n   415\tan outer `append!` must fit the outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   416\t\n   417\t## 4. Reachability and NAT\n   418\t\n   419\tA channel needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n   420\tcoordinated simultaneous send. The protocol cannot change that; it makes\n   421\tthe workarounds conventions. One peer, M below, is reachable and serves two\n   422\tordinary streams from its table; which peer that is carries no protocol\n   423\tmeaning, and any reachable peer can take its place.\n   424\t\n   425\t- One side receives unsolicited (public address, forwarded port, or\n   426\t  endpoint-independent mapping and filtering): direct; the other side dials,\n   427\t  WebSocket or UDP.\n   428\t- Endpoint-dependent mapping or filtering on one or both sides, punching\n   429\t  still possible: hole punch through M (below), UDP allowed.\n   430\t- Endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a symmetric NAT;\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
