{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '58,82p;128,139p;165,184p;210,229p;252,268p;486,558p'\"","aggregated_output":"    58\t## 2. The design: mirror and reflection\n    59\t\n    60\tOne sentence: a peer that holds a handle answers the handle's own operations\n    61\tas data over a channel; a peer at the other end holds a handle whose\n    62\toperations are those questions asked across the channel, with the source's\n    63\toutcome maps returned verbatim. Four concepts.\n    64\t\n    65\t- **channel**: two ordinary handles with exactly two ends, a writer whose\n    66\t  `append!` carries one value toward the other end and a reader positioned\n    67\t  on the values the other end sent; built from a WebSocket attachment and\n    68\t  its projection (3.1), a UDP socket adapter and its deposit medium (3.2),\n    69\t  two ring buffers in one process, or two reflections served by a third\n    70\t  peer (3.3).\n    71\t- **table**: composition data on a peer, `{identity {:handle h :surface\n    72\t  S}}` plus the optional keys of sections 6 and 7. A plain map the\n    73\t  composition owns, not a registry (`dao.stream.md`, No hidden global\n    74\t  state).\n    75\t- **mirror step**: the pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer\n    76\t  requests against its table. It holds no state between calls beyond the\n    77\t  channel reader's cursor.\n    78\t- **reflection**: the handle `attach!` returns for a remote descriptor. A\n    79\t  **link**, one per channel, holds what the reflections on that channel\n    80\t  share.\n    81\t\n    82\tEvery peer may run any number of mirror steps and hold any number of\n   128\tconsumes a descriptor and produces a local handle.\n   129\t\n   130\t**Protocol errors.** The mirror answers three errors with the error key,\n   131\tnever by fabricating an outcome the operation's closed set forbids\n   132\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention; Surfaces):\n   133\t\n   134\t- `not-found`: the identity is absent from the table, whether never served,\n   135\t  retired by policy, or reclaimed with its lease. An identity this peer does\n   136\t  not serve has no operations and no surface, so this one error is the\n   137\t  honest answer for all four ops.\n   138\t- `no-surface`: the op is outside the entry's declared surface, under the\n   139\t  fixed mapping: `cursor` and `next` need `:reader`, `append!` needs\n   165\t`:dao.stream/attach` entry for `:dao.stream/remote` and no\n   166\t`:dao.stream/create` entry.\n   167\t\n   168\t### 2.3 The mirror step\n   169\t\n   170\t`(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) -> cursor'`. For each\n   171\trequest read from `chan-reader`, in order, bounded by a composition budget:\n   172\t\n   173\t1. Look up `:dao.stream/identity` in the table. Absent: append the answer\n   174\t   with `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/not-found`.\n   175\t2. Present, but the op outside the entry's declared surface under 2.1's\n   176\t   mapping: append the answer with\n   177\t   `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/no-surface`.\n   178\t3. Otherwise answer `:dao.stream/descriptor` from the entry's handle\n   179\t   directly, adding `:dao.stream.remote/surface`; and apply\n   180\t   `:dao.stream/cursor`, `:dao.stream/next` and `:dao.stream/append!` to it\n   181\t   through `dao.stream.middleware/apply-request`\n   182\t   (`dao.stream.middleware.md`), with context\n   183\t   `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <channel attachment identity>}`. The budget\n   184\t   chase below runs the same `next` through the same path.\n   210\t### 2.4 The reflection\n   211\t\n   212\t`attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\n   213\treflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\n   214\tcontract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\n   215\tThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n   216\tshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\n   217\twriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n   218\toutstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\n   219\tkeyed by id; an optional event writer; and the policy datum\n   220\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`.\n   221\t\n   222\tOn attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\n   223\tanswer is the reflection's confirmation. The `not-found` error marks the\n   224\treflection **gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and declared\n   225\tsurface. Either is appended to the event writer when one is composed.\n   226\t\n   227\t**Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\n   228\treader to `blocked`, filing each answer whose id is outstanding, installing\n   229\teach `:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\n   252\t  elsewhere. The source's outcome is appended to the event writer when it\n   253\t  is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is dropped.\n   254\t- `close!`: local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards\n   255\t  `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n   256\t  filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.\n   257\t\n   258\t**Protocol errors at the reflection.** A filed `not-found` marks the\n   259\treflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n   260\tanswers `transport-error` with\n   261\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, while\n   262\t`descriptor` still answers `ok`: a name outlives what it named. A filed\n   263\t`no-surface` or `oversize` marks nothing; each is returned, for the\n   264\toperation that caused it, as `transport-error` with\n   265\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason` set to the error's name. A reflection marked\n   266\tgone is how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6). A request send\n   267\tthe channel writer refuses with `full` (establishment, back pressure)\n   268\tleaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered:\n   486\tthe drain files the source's `ok` with `c0`. `next c0` proceeds the same\n   487\tway: `blocked`, then `ok \"hello\" c1`, and `next c1` is `end`. Nothing knew\n   488\tit was a string.\n   489\t\n   490\t**Request and response service.** A peer S enters `requests` with surface\n   491\t`#{:writer}` and `answers` with surface `#{:reader}` in its table and runs\n   492\tan interpreter over the local ends; a peer P attaches reflections to both,\n   493\tmints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n   494\t`requests`, and reads `answers` until it observes `:svc/request r`. Request\n   495\tids are self-minted random values so callers sharing one `answers` stream\n   496\tdo not collide; a caller that needs private answers asks S for a pair. S is\n   497\ta \"server\" only in that its table has those entries and its interpreter has\n   498\tthat policy, and either peer may serve such a pair to the other over the\n   499\tsame channel. This is Linda's request medium, and the shape `yin.repl` and\n   500\tcontent lookup take (implementation plan, section 1). The meeting board and\n   501\tthe relay pair are section 4's instances of it.\n   502\t\n   503\t**Peer names.** A name is a self-certifying hash of a public key, the\n   504\tkickoff-hash form of `dao.stream.discovery.md`. It appears only inside\n   505\tconvention payloads. Nothing authenticates it until authentication exists.\n   506\t\n   507\t## 6. Lease integration\n   508\t\n   509\tA served stream is a network resource, so its lifetime beyond one call is\n   510\tgoverned by `dao.lease.md`. The judge is composed inside the peer that\n   511\tpossesses the resource, as that contract requires. What a lease governs:\n   512\t\n   513\t- A table entry a peer serves by its own policy: no lease; the peer retires\n   514\t  it when it likes.\n   515\t- A table entry served for a remote party (a stream a migrated continuation\n   516\t  still reads): yes; the remote party holds it, the holding peer grants and\n   517\t  judges, the identity is the subject.\n   518\t- A relay pair on a meeting peer: yes; the requesting peer holds it, the\n   519\t  meeting peer grants and judges, the two identities are the subject.\n   520\t- Link state, UDP reassembly buffers, channel sockets: no; governed by close\n   521\t  or by a bounded buffer.\n   522\t- NAT mappings and punch state: no; possessed by the NAT box, so no judge\n   523\t  can exist, and keep-alive cadence is the peer's own (section 4).\n   524\t- Board postings: no; records under the board's retention, each carrying the\n   525\t  lease id of the registration or pair it belongs to, and a reader treats a\n   526\t  posting whose lease has a `:lapsed` on the board as stale.\n   527\t\n   528\t**Mechanism: no new wire shape.** A grantor enters two conventional streams\n   529\tin its table, `lease-proposals` with surface `#{:writer}` and\n   530\t`lease-grants` with surface `#{:reader}`, published as remote descriptors\n   531\tbeside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\n   532\treflection on the first and reads its grant through a reflection on the\n   533\tsecond. The grant creates one more table entry, a ring buffer with surface\n   534\t`#{:writer}`, and names its identity as `:dao.lease/holder`: this is the\n   535\tholder's **renewal medium**, and whatever lands on it is attributed to the\n   536\tholder by the medium, the attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` names as\n   537\tper-author media. The judge reads it with a local cursor. The renewal\n   538\tmedium lives and dies with its lease; the reclaim procedure removes the\n   539\tsubject entries and the renewal entry together, idempotently, and reports\n   540\tsuccess. A table entry under lease carries `:dao.lease/lease L`.\n   541\t\n   542\tUntil ShiBi exists, a renewal medium is reachable by anyone who learns its\n   543\tdescriptor and accepts a third party's renewal, extending a lease it does\n   544\tnot hold: attribution needs no credential, extension may want one, and a\n   545\tcomposition that must not accept it composes a gate on the renewal entry.\n   546\t\n   547\t**Renewal.** `append!` of `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal\n   548\t:dao.lease/lease L}` on the holder's reflection of its renewal medium. The\n   549\treflection's `ok` is outbound acceptance only; the holder advances its\n   550\trenewal bound only on the source's `ok` observed on the link's event\n   551\twriter. A holder with no event writer stops at its bound early, never late.\n   552\t\n   553\t**Reclaim observed.** The mirror answers the `not-found` protocol error for\n   554\ta reclaimed identity and the reflection marks itself gone (2.4). The holder\n   555\tmay also read the grantor's own `:lapsed` record through its reflection on\n   556\t`lease-grants`: that is observing the grantor's stream, not carriage. And\n   557\tthe holder stops at its own bound regardless.\n   558\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"The active JVM sketch has two distinct checks: lease lapse closes a real copy-model serving session, and the holder sees that closure. I’m checking which assertions can map directly to a leased mirror entry and a reflection marked gone."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc | sed -n '677,865p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc | sed -n '677,865p'\"","aggregated_output":"   677\t(deftest served-connection-lifetime-sketch-test\n   678\t  (testing \"sketch: the served connection lifetime -- envelope-key\n   679\t            attribution and :lapsed carriage (C8)\"\n   680\t    ;; Attribution by ENVELOPE KEY (D4; the :ws/attachment envelope key of\n   681\t    ;; ws.cljc:167-174): each fact carries its attachment id in the\n   682\t    ;; envelope, and the resolver reads it FROM THE FACT -- the wired\n   683\t    ;; source is never consulted, which is exactly what declaring\n   684\t    ;; :attribution :envelope-key means (r4-P2: the earlier sketch\n   685\t    ;; declared :attachment-identity while resolving like per-author\n   686\t    ;; media, and nothing noticed).\n   687\t    ;;\n   688\t    ;; OWED (r4-r3/F2, recorded by the orchestrator in the plan's §6):\n   689\t    ;; the flattened shape modelled here -- :ws/attachment sitting\n   690\t    ;; directly on the lease fact -- is NOT the transport's. ws.cljc\n   691\t    ;; deposits {:ws/attachment <id> :ws/event ... :ws/value <payload>};\n   692\t    ;; a real composition unwraps :ws/value before the judge sees a\n   693\t    ;; lease fact, and the envelope key, as modelled here, is\n   694\t    ;; SELF-ASSERTED: whoever appends to the medium chooses it. The\n   695\t    ;; transport's authoritativeness comes from the connection, not the\n   696\t    ;; key -- an unwrap seam and a trusted binding are owed work.\n   697\t    (let [connection (atom {:open? true})\n   698\t          traffic (new-buffer 16)\n   699\t          holder-reads (new-buffer 16)\n   700\t          envelope-resolver (fn [_source fact] (get fact :ws/attachment))\n   701\t          medium (assoc standard-medium :attribution :envelope-key)\n   702\t          system (composed\n   703\t                   {:resolver envelope-resolver\n   704\t                    :resolver-bindings #{:envelope-key}\n   705\t                    :medium medium\n   706\t                    :reclaim (fn [_subject]\n   707\t                               (swap! connection assoc :open? false)\n   708\t                               true)})\n   709\t          grant (lease/grant :l1 :socket-handle :holder-a {:ms 10})\n   710\t          system (-> system\n   711\t                     (update :judge lease/author-grant grant)\n   712\t                     (tick! 1)\n   713\t                     step!)\n   714\t          ;; the holder's renewal rides its envelope: :ws/attachment\n   715\t          system (-> system\n   716\t                     (fact! (assoc (lease/renewal :l1)\n   717\t                                   :ws/attachment :holder-a))\n   718\t                     (tick! 7)\n   719\t                     step!)]\n   720\t      (is (= {:ms 7} (get-in system [:judge :ledger :l1 :last-observation]))\n   721\t          \"the envelope-keyed renewal counted as :holder-a's evidence -- the\n   722\t           resolver read the author from the envelope, not the source\")\n   723\t      (let [system (-> system (tick! 18) step!)]\n   724\t        (is (false? (:open? @connection))\n   725\t            \"reclaim closed the served connection\")\n   726\t        (is (= [(lease/lapsed :l1 :silence)] (records system))\n   727\t            \"the :lapsed record is on the grantor's writer\")\n   728\t        ;; CARRIAGE: the composition's step copying grantor facts to the\n   729\t        ;; holder's inbound medium, with :lapsed filtered out. The writer\n   730\t        ;; HELD a :lapsed; the holder's medium must not receive it.\n   731\t        (let [carry! (carriage (:writer system) holder-reads)]\n   732\t          (carry!)\n   733\t          (is (= [grant] (drain-values holder-reads))\n   734\t              \"the grant was carried across; the :lapsed was filtered: the\n   735\t               holder observes the reclaim as the connection's closing --\n   736\t               the resource event -- never as the grantor's record\"))))))\n   737\t\n   738\t#?(:cljd nil\n   739\t   :clj\n   740\t   (deftest served-connection-real-close-path-sketch-test\n   741\t     (testing \"sketch (JVM): one real serving session, reclaimed through the\n   742\t               real close path\"\n   743\t       ;; The served-connection sketch against the real transport: one\n   744\t       ;; ws endpoint, one accepted connection, the serving driver\n   745\t       ;; stepping it. The lease's subject is the served stream; the\n   746\t       ;; reclaim is the real close -- close the served stream, then one\n   747\t       ;; serving step retires the session and runs the WebSocket close\n   748\t       ;; (:close-ended!).\n   749\t       ;;\n   750\t       ;; OWED (r4-r3/F2, recorded by the orchestrator in the plan's\n   751\t       ;; §6): the envelope shape flattened onto the renewal below is\n   752\t       ;; not the transport's -- ws.cljc deposits {:ws/attachment <id>\n   753\t       ;; :ws/event ... :ws/value <payload>}, so a real composition\n   754\t       ;; unwraps :ws/value before the judge sees a lease fact, and the\n   755\t       ;; :ws/attachment key, as modelled here, is SELF-ASSERTED:\n   756\t       ;; whoever appends to the traffic medium chooses it. The\n   757\t       ;; transport's authoritativeness is the connection's, not the\n   758\t       ;; key's -- an unwrap seam and a trusted binding are owed work.\n   759\t       (let [service (buffer 16)\n   760\t             traffic (buffer 16)\n   761\t             {:keys [endpoint composition ended]}\n   762\t             (serving-fixture {:service service :traffic traffic})\n   763\t             accepted (ws/accept-connection! endpoint \"/lease/sketch\"\n   764\t                                             {:send! (fn [_])\n   765\t                                              :close! (fn [& _] nil)}\n   766\t                                             10)\n   767\t             attachment (:ws/attachment accepted)\n   768\t             writer (new-buffer 16)\n   769\t             ticks (buffer 8)\n   770\t             envelope-resolver (fn [_source fact]\n   771\t                                 (get fact :ws/attachment))\n   772\t             composed-judge\n   773\t             (lease/make-judge\n   774\t               {:cadence {:ms 5}\n   775\t                :units {:ms 1}\n   776\t                :tolerance {:ms 0}\n   777\t                :resolver envelope-resolver\n   778\t                :resolver-bindings #{:envelope-key}\n   779\t                :reclaim (fn [_subject]\n   780\t                           (= :dao.stream/ok\n   781\t                              (:dao.stream/outcome (ds/close! service))))\n   782\t                :writer writer\n   783\t                :self :grantor\n   784\t                :ticks [{:handle ticks :cursor (oldest-cursor ticks)}]\n   785\t                :media [{:handle traffic\n   786\t                         :cursor (oldest-cursor traffic)\n   787\t                         :source :traffic\n   788\t                         :medium (assoc standard-medium\n   789\t                                        :attribution :envelope-key)}]})\n   790\t             judge (atom (lease/author-grant\n   791\t                          (:judge composed-judge)\n   792\t                          (lease/grant :l1 :served-session attachment {:ms 10})))\n   793\t             judge-tick! (fn [ms]\n   794\t                           (append-ok! ticks (lease/tick {:ms ms})))\n   795\t             step-judge! (fn [] (reset! judge ((:step composed-judge) @judge)))]\n   796\t         (serving/start! composition)\n   797\t         (serving/step! composition 10)\n   798\t         (is (contains? (:sessions (serving/state composition)) attachment)\n   799\t             \"the session was accepted and is being served\")\n   800\t         (judge-tick! 1) (step-judge!)\n   801\t         ;; the holder's renewal rides its attachment envelope\n   802\t         (append-ok! traffic (assoc (lease/renewal :l1)\n   803\t                                    :ws/attachment attachment))\n   804\t         (judge-tick! 7) (step-judge!)\n   805\t         (is (= {:ms 7} (get-in @judge [:ledger :l1 :last-observation]))\n   806\t             \"the renewal counted through the real traffic medium\")\n   807\t         ;; silence past duration: the reclaim closes the served stream\n   808\t         (judge-tick! 18) (step-judge!)\n   809\t         (is (= [(lease/lapsed :l1 :silence)]\n   810\t                (rest (drain-values writer)))\n   811\t             \"the lease lapsed and was recorded\")\n   812\t         ;; one serving step drives the real close: the session retires\n   813\t         ;; and the WebSocket close runs\n   814\t         (serving/step! composition 19)\n   815\t         (is (empty? (:sessions (serving/state composition)))\n   816\t             \"the session was retired by the real close path\")\n   817\t         (is (= 1 (count @ended))\n   818\t             \"and the WebSocket close (:close-ended!) ran\")))))\n   819\t\n   820\t\n   821\t(deftest shared-work-claim-sketch-test\n   822\t  (testing \"sketch: the shared-work claim -- a composition-supplied\n   823\t            reclaim following call-close!'s idempotency\"\n   824\t    ;; A shared-work claim's reclaim is a composition-supplied procedure\n   825\t    ;; in call-close!'s idempotency shape (serving.cljc:38-43): the first\n   826\t    ;; invocation performs the release and reports success; later\n   827\t    ;; invocations see the work already released and report success again\n   828\t    ;; without acting twice -- which is what the judge's pending-retry\n   829\t    ;; path demands of every reclaim it may invoke more than once.\n   830\t    (let [work (atom {:released? false})\n   831\t          releases (atom 0)\n   832\t          reclaim (fn [_subject]\n   833\t                    (when-not (:released? @work)\n   834\t                      (swap! work assoc :released? true)\n   835\t                      (swap! releases inc))\n   836\t                    true)\n   837\t          system (composed {:reclaim reclaim})\n   838\t          system (-> system\n   839\t                     (update :judge lease/author-grant\n   840\t                             (lease/grant :l1 :shared-work :holder-a {:ms 10}))\n   841\t                     (tick! 1)\n   842\t                     step!)\n   843\t          ;; the writer fails before the record, so the lease sits\n   844\t          ;; pending and the next pass invokes the procedure AGAIN\n   845\t          system (-> system\n   846\t                     (update :judge assoc :writer\n   847\t                             (fake-writer :dao.stream/full))\n   848\t                     (tick! 12)\n   849\t                     step!)\n   850\t          system (-> system\n   851\t                     (update :judge assoc :writer (:writer system))\n   852\t                     (tick! 13)\n   853\t                     step!)]\n   854\t      (is (= 1 @releases)\n   855\t          \"two reclaim invocations, one release: the procedure is idempotent\")\n   856\t      (is (true? (:released? @work)))\n   857\t      (is (= [(lease/lapsed :l1 :silence)] (records system))\n   858\t          \"the retry recorded after the writer recovered\"))))\n   859\t\n   860\t\n   861\t;; C5/C6/C7 are pinned outside the runtime tests, as the plan's §7 states:\n   862\t;; no timer or clock call exists in src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc (the grep),\n   863\t;; dao.lease requires dao.stream and never the reverse (by construction),\n   864\t;; and no machinery renews on a holder's behalf (the holder section has no\n   865\t;; renewal-appending function at all -- appending is the control flow's\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-27 10:47:38 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-27 17:47:38 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Ruling: Option A — migrate the JVM sketch.** Its useful proof is that a lease judge reclaims a served resource and the holder can observe that loss. The current test reaches that result through `dao.stream.serving` session retirement and WebSocket close, the copy path slice 5 deletes. It also models renewal attribution with a self-asserted, flattened `:ws/attachment` value; the test itself records that this is not the transport’s shape ([test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:740](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:740), [test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:750](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:750), [test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:796](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:796)). The plan retires `dao.stream.serving` in slice 5 ([docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:103](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:103), [docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:188](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:188)).\n\n**Slice 5 replacement:** Keep one active lease composition test, using two in-process ring buffers as a channel, a mirror table, and a holder reflection. Enter one served ring-buffer handle under identity `I`, surface `#{:reader}`, and lease `L`; grant `L` with subject `I`. Wire a separate per-author renewal medium to the judge. After the initial tick, assert the holder’s reflection can resolve and read the served entry. Append a renewal to that medium, step the judge at the existing reading of 7, and assert `:last-observation` is `{:ms 7}`. At reading 18, have the judge’s idempotent reclaim remove `I` from the table and report success. Assert the entry is absent, the grantor writer contains exactly `(lease/lapsed L :silence)`, and the ledger no longer contains `L`. Drive one more reflection request and mirror step; assert the mirror answers `:dao.stream.remote/not-found`, the reflection becomes gone, and a subsequent handle operation returns `:dao.stream/transport-error` with reason `:dao.stream.remote/not-found`. These are the replacement for the old session and WebSocket-close assertions ([test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:803](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:803), [docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:71](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:71), [docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:173](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:173), [docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:258](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:258)).\n\nUse the specified per-author medium rather than carrying over the test’s flattened attachment-key assertion: section 6 makes that medium the holder’s renewal path and requires reclaim to remove the served and renewal entries together ([docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:528](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:528)). Remove the obsolete `serving-fixture`, its private fixture values, and the `dao.stream.serving` require if no other test uses them. Keep the separate carriage sketch’s grant and `:lapsed` assertions; a lapse remains the grantor’s record, while a holder observes reclaim through the reflection ([test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:677](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc:677), [docs/design/dao.lease.md:289](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:289), [docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:553](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:553)). No new slice-7 proof row is needed for this single-entry lifetime; slice 7 already covers leased relay pairs ([docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:114](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md:114)).\n\nNo files were edited.\n\nStatus: COMPLETE"}}
